diagnosis_text:
dswa1 — the Spanning Tree Protocol root bridge (priority 0x1000) for the A-side distribution block — has a misconfigured secondary IP address on its management-VLAN bridge sub-interface `br0.99`. The interface carries both the correct address `10.0.0.2/28` and a spurious secondary `10.0.0.1/32`. Because FRR/Zebra sees two distinct addresses on the same interface, the OSPF daemon creates two parallel interface instances for br0.99: the valid `/28` instance (which successfully forms the Full adjacency with dswa2) and an "unnumbered" `/32` instance (which sends hello packets advertising a `/32` network mask). Per RFC 2328 §10.5, dswa2 rejects every hello from the `/32` instance (mask mismatch: configured /28, received /32), logging the error at every 10-second hello interval. The spurious address is simultaneously re-distributed as a host route (`10.0.0.1/32`) into OSPF, polluting the routing tables of csw1, csw2, and all downstream routers. The dual OSPF instance state on dswa1 destabilises the FRR ospfd process, causing it to crash and restart periodically (last observed restart: 09:56:12, which triggered the most recent campus freeze). On each FRR restart: Zebra resets all bridge interfaces; the Linux bridge STP state machine resets; all br0 ports cycle through listen → learn → forward (30 s blackout); OSPF adjacencies expire after their 40-second dead interval; and the combined routing and L2 outage lasts 60–120 seconds. **Fix**: remove the spurious address with `ip addr del 10.0.0.1/32 dev br0.99` on dswa1, persist the removal in the device startup configuration, and restart FRR to clear the duplicate OSPF interface state.
=== END ===